// Self-host Stage 3: constant REMATERIALIZATION on ARM64 — split out of
// compiler/arm64dispatch.bit (#4666) at the 800-line limit. Sibling .bit file,
// same module: see arm64.bit for the backend overview.
//
// One question, asked in three places that must agree, which is why they sit
// together rather than beside each of their callers:
//
//   * `isRematConst` — is this vreg's whole definition a `mov xN, #imm` that
//     `movImm64` emits in ONE word? The predicate, and nothing else reads
//     `Op`/`movImm64Words` for this purpose.
//   * `markRematIntervals` — tells the ALLOCATOR, before it runs, so
//     `preferSpill` can evict such an interval first and `assignSpill` can
//     refuse it a stack slot (regalloc.bit / regallocspill.bit).
//   * `rematSpilledConst` / `addRematSkips` — tells EMISSION, after it ran:
//     the definition materializes nothing, and each use recomputes the value.
//
// A disagreement between the first two and the third is not a slow program: the
// allocator would withhold a slot the emitter then addresses, and
// `spillOffset`'s assert (arm64ctx.bit) is what turns that into a build
// failure rather than a read of a frame word nothing ever wrote.

// #4636. Whether `vreg`'s whole definition is a one-word `mov xN, #imm`.
//
// THE BAR IS ONE INSTRUCTION, and it is not a tuning knob. A reload is one
// `ldr`, so a one-word `movImm64` is never worse on instruction count and
// strictly better on memory traffic; two words would ADD an instruction per
// use to remove a load, a loss in the issue-width-bound loops this backend is
// measured on and a win only in the memory-stall-bound ones. One word is the
// trade that holds under either regime. It is wider than it looks: MOVN covers
// a 51-bit mask (0x7ffffffffffff is `movn xN, #0xfff8, lsl #48`), the exact
// shape #4281 gives `_m8$fe25519MulCore` a function-long live range.
//
// `Op.ConstFloat` is deliberately out: `emitConstFloat` (arm64selectfp.bit)
// has no FP immediate form and always emits `movImm64` PLUS an `fmov`, so a
// float remat is two words against a one-word `ldr` — never the trade above.
// `Op.ConstNil` is out for a different reason: `emitConstNil` materializes a
// hardcoded 0 and never reads `ins.ival`, so `constIntValue` is not its value.
//
// Reading `f.instrs[vreg]` is the SAME identity `regLocOf` (arm64select.bit)
// already rests on: `buildIntervals` (codegenlive.bit) gives instruction i the
// interval whose vreg is i, so `result.locations` and `f.instrs` index one
// space.
fn isRematConst(f: IrFunc, vreg: int): bool {
  let ins = f.instrs[vreg]
  if (!opIsIntConst(ins.op)) {
    return false
  }
  return movImm64Words(constIntValue(ins)) <= 1
}

// #4636. A SPILLED value whose whole definition is `mov xN, #imm` is
// recomputed at the use instead of reloaded: emits the materialization into
// `dst` and answers true, or answers false and leaves `getInt`
// (arm64select.bit) to load the slot exactly as before.
//
// The other half of the question `computeSkipConstMaterialize`
// (arm64dispatch.bit) asks. That one names a constant no register ever holds
// because every use folds it into an immediate; this one names a constant no
// register COULD hold — the allocator has already put it on the stack — and
// recomputes it, which costs the one instruction that defined it and touches
// no memory.
//
// Correctness here is a value equality and not a liveness argument — the value
// is exactly what `emitConstInt` would have materialized, `movImm64` of
// `constIntValue(ins)`, and this emits that same call into the same register
// the `ldr` would have written. MOVZ/MOVN/MOVK set no flags and neither does
// LDR, so a fused compare between a read and its branch cannot tell them apart.
fn rematSpilledConst(cx: A64Ctx, vreg: int, dst: int): bool {
  if (!isRematConst(cx.f, vreg)) {
    return false
  }
  movImm64(cx.code, dst, constIntValue(cx.f.instrs[vreg]))
  return true
}

// #4666. Marks every interval the allocator may evict for free, BEFORE it runs.
//
// `isRef` and the register class are re-checked rather than assumed: a
// remat interval owns no stack slot, so `allocateCoalesced` refuses one that
// could reach the GC stack maps or the float slot-number shift, and refusing
// here keeps that assert a statement about the allocator's contract instead of
// a way to discover this function was wrong. Neither can fire today —
// `opIsIntConst` types are integers and integers are Int-class non-references —
// so this costs two comparisons on the constants of a function and buys the
// property that a future `opIsIntConst` widening cannot silently publish a
// slot that was never written.
fn markRematIntervals(f: IrFunc, intervals: []LiveInterval) {
  let i = 0
  while (i < len(intervals)) {
    let iv = intervals[i]
    if (iv.regClass == RegClass.Int && !iv.isRef && isRematConst(f, iv.vreg)) {
      intervals[i].remat = true
    }
    i = i + 1
  }
}

// #4666. Widens `computeSkipConstMaterialize`'s answer with the constants the
// allocator has just refused a slot: their definitions materialize nothing
// either.
//
// The two reasons a constant's definition emits no instruction are different
// and both end here. `skip` means "every use folds this into an immediate, so
// no register is needed"; this adds "no register was available and no slot was
// taken, so every use recomputes it". `arm64WritesResult` reads the union and
// is right for both — neither binds a register.
//
// Indexed by instruction id, the same space `locations` is indexed by (see
// `isRematConst` for that identity), so the two arrays line up element for
// element.
fn addRematSkips(skip: []bool, locations: []RegLoc) {
  assert(len(skip) == len(locations))
  let i = 0
  while (i < len(skip)) {
    if (locations[i].isSpill && locations[i].index < 0) {
      skip[i] = true
    }
    i = i + 1
  }
}
